From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:08:54 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 237/342] multi_audio: at least four buffers for an output

The buffers were as many as the consumer's latency spans, plus two;
BMediaRecorder's node says it has none, so a microphone recorded into
two buffers of 128 ms: when the recording was late now and then (NeoCam
at 1920x1080), the next buffer couldn't be had in time and its sound was
lost (0.25 to 0.6 s in a 10 s clip). Four now at least.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../media/media-add-ons/multi_audio/MultiAudioNode.cpp     | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/add-ons/media/media-add-ons/multi_audio/MultiAudioNode.cpp b/src/add-ons/media/media-add-ons/multi_audio/MultiAudioNode.cpp
index 7cb3ec8ef1..8e0ba06921 100644
--- a/src/add-ons/media/media-add-ons/multi_audio/MultiAudioNode.cpp
+++ b/src/add-ons/media/media-add-ons/multi_audio/MultiAudioNode.cpp
@@ -2074,9 +2074,14 @@ MultiAudioNode::_AllocateBuffers(node_output &channel)
 {
 	CALLED();
 
-	// allocate enough buffers to span our downstream latency, plus one
+	// allocate enough buffers to span our downstream latency, plus one; at
+	// least four: a consumer that says it has no latency (BMediaRecorder)
+	// may still be late now and then, and with two buffers that lost
+	// sound (a buffer not given back in time is one not recorded)
 	size_t size = channel.fOutput.format.u.raw_audio.buffer_size;
 	int32 count = int32(fLatency / BufferDuration() + 1 + 1);
+	if (count < 4)
+		count = 4;
 
 	PRINT(("\tlatency = %" B_PRIdBIGTIME ", buffer duration = %" B_PRIdBIGTIME
 			"\n", fLatency, BufferDuration()));
